5742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "396a0949-2648-520d-b31c-049c70bbd0c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167807 #edb/ref 1061 45745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2dac84d2-443e-546b-baa3-7a9ee2e2ef2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167808 #edb/ref 1061 45748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3892412b-7fe5-5337-9881-d9cb02031080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167809 #edb/ref 1061 45749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3eb3250a-641e-50c7-85ee-a4a11b6073b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168154 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the *maximum* value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 + 4x_2^2,\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraint $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$ This type of problem is known as a **constrained optimization problem**.   \n\nSince our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ has no mixed term $x_1x_2$, we can find its maximum value for $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1$ using algebraic manipulation, as follows.\n\nFirst, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 4x_1^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to make sure that $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can actually attain the value $4.$ Can we find a vector $\\mathbf x,$ with  $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1,$ such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 4?$\n\nIndeed, we can. Setting ![](images/af/afe399f75b9a73a1834b4d359e29ad4814bd99f81b1cf20ccaa211078dd19645.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$  the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ equals $4.$\n\nProceeding similarly, we can show that the minimum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $2.$ \n\nGraphically, the situation can be visualized if we intersect the surface $z= {\\color{black}2x_1^2+4x_2^2}$ and the cylinder $\\color{black}x_1^2+x_2^2=1,$ as shown below:  \n\n![](images/39/39bfc05c9dd0733c1f4f867d21d1a5f018aacbf0ed91036a6f0981d701ba69d8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168154 #edb/ref 1065 7952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168154 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf2f75a6-c5dc-5dc6-bcc2-1c89fec829de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168154 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168155 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find a quadratic form's maximum and minimum values even when it contains cross-product terms. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin{:}$*\n\n- *The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n*The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$ *\n\n- *The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n*The minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$ *\n\nSuppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=4x_1^2-2x_1x_2+4x_2^2.\n$$\n\nHow can we find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ at which the maximum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is attained?\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/0a/0af81fbf910d2f2bbe2ad26f297f57ce230548e13ad27c2974109066b9faaee5.svg)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = 5.\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}},$ we need a unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5.$ We start by computing $(A-5I)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/a2/a257df47b352c5534608079419a337ae51103dd7c7763fb73f4fd692f36fd47e.svg)\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n![](images/cb/cb08ba808e7b6d8a8412df8cafaa85e795f523b63450d8f4e1c3081740a46866.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168155 #edb/ref 1065 7953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168155 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "10db6c75-237c-5a28-b5a8-ac25c57cc82f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168155 #edb/ref 1067 "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266424 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266424 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266424 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fddd54e-2f69-5317-9273-59acf0420171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266792 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266792 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266792 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "488b4f03-ec81-5e5e-88ac-1e1b03de77fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267099 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267099 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c2ad35c-a214-5c83-bfd9-e7a6a53c9023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267872 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c18a481a-15ba-5133-9b96-62027df826c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268394 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff581e6d-531a-50a8-93f1-45b62be1bbc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186267872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268695 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2163251-a2b2-5338-af76-79765630ea17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2bb923d-a428-53a1-96e5-4cae59c3d2d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269164 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -2.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-2.$ But before we conclude that $-2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275490 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275490 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275490 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "018c99a3-0395-5f65-a414-0b527b9b1fb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275795 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275795 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275795 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2418fd71-994a-57d9-8058-e7d13fe938ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276512 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186